{{define "intro" -}}
This folder is the **Backyard** site `{{.Site}}`. Backyard publishes a folder of static files
(HTML, CSS, JS, images) to an internal URL that only people in the company can open, and gives
every page a zero-config backend through one script: `backyard.db` (JSON documents with
realtime queries), `backyard.files` (uploads), `backyard.ai` (LLM chat and streaming, images,
embeddings), `backyard.warehouse` (read-only SQL), `backyard.socket` (websocket rooms with
presence) and `backyard.id` (the signed-in user).
{{- if .Server}}

- Backyard server (hub): {{.Server}}
- This site once deployed: {{if .SiteURL}}{{.SiteURL}}{{else}}{{if .HostURL}}{{.HostURL}} (host mode) or {{end}}{{.PathURL}} (path mode){{end}}; `backyard deploy` prints the exact URL.
{{- end}}
{{- end}}

{{define "rules" -}}
## Rules

1. **Static files only.** `index.html` is the entry point. No server code (no Node/Python/PHP
   backends, API routes or cron jobs): use the built-in APIs. A build step is optional; deploy
   its output folder (`backyard deploy dist`).
2. **Load the SDK with an absolute path**, before your code:
   `<script src="/__backyard/backyard.js"></script>`. Reference this site's own files with
   **relative** paths (`app.js`, `img/logo.png`), never `/app.js`.
3. **Never put secrets in any file** (API keys, tokens, passwords): everything deployed is
   readable by every employee. The server holds the AI and warehouse credentials.
4. **All data is shared by everyone who can open the site**; there are no per-user
   permissions. Use `_meta.created_by` only for UX such as "mine".
5. **Identity is built in:** `await backyard.me()`. Never build login forms or ask for names.
6. **Realtime by default:** render shared data with `collection.watch(render, query)` so every
   open tab updates live; use rooms for presence, cursors and typing indicators.
7. **Self-contained and robust:** vendor libraries into this folder instead of using public
   CDNs; insert user content with `textContent`; handle loading, empty and error states
   (`unsupported` means AI or the warehouse is not enabled on the server).
8. **Deploy = overwrite.** `backyard deploy` replaces the whole site `{{.Site}}` (its data
   survives). Deploy when the user asks for it, and confirm before deploying to another site
   name.
9. **Ship a preview for the hub.** Keep the `<title>` and `<meta name="description">` of
   `index.html` accurate, and add a `preview.svg` next to it: a 1280×720 sketch of the app's
   main screen in its own colors (`viewBox="0 0 1280 720"`, system fonts, no scripts or
   external files, at most 1 MiB). The hub shows it on the site's card.
{{- end}}

{{define "commands" -}}
## Commands

```sh
backyard dev                  # preview at http://{{.Site}}.localhost:4321/ with a local backend (long-running: start it in the background)
backyard deploy               # publish this folder (overwrites the site; prints its URL)
backyard deploy --dry-run     # show what would be uploaded and deleted
backyard sites list [filter]  # existing sites; `backyard pull <site>` downloads one to study or reuse
```

- Check the running preview with curl: `curl -s -H 'Host: {{.Site}}.localhost:4321' http://127.0.0.1:4321/`.
  Outside browsers `*.localhost` often does not resolve (Node `fetch`, wget and Python fail in
  containers and CI), and `http://localhost:4321/` is the dev hub, not this site.
- The site name is in `.backyard.json`. Never deployed: `CLAUDE.md`, `AGENTS.md`, `.claude/`,
  `.cursor/`, `.github/`, `node_modules/`, dotfiles (including `.backyard-data/`, the local
  data of `backyard dev`) and the patterns in `.backyardignore`.
- If `backyard deploy` says you are not logged in, ask the user to run
  `backyard login{{if .Server}} --server {{.Server}}{{else}} --server <hub URL>{{end}}` in a terminal (it opens the browser), then retry.
{{- if not .Server}}
  No Backyard server yet? `backyard cloudflare setup` (run by the user; it opens a browser) deploys
  one to their own Cloudflare account on the free plan and logs the CLI in. It needs a release
  build of the CLI (`install.sh`): one from `go install` needs a checkout of the Backyard
  repository or `--package`.
{{- end}}
- AI and warehouse calls answer `unsupported` under a plain `backyard dev`; they work with
  `backyard dev --config backyard.yaml` (providers configured) or once deployed.
{{- end}}

{{define "api" -}}
## Backyard API cheat sheet

Methods return Promises unless marked sync. Failures throw a `BackyardError` with `err.code`
(`bad_request`, `unauthenticated`, `forbidden`, `not_found`, `conflict`, `too_large`,
`rate_limited`, `unsupported`, `upstream_error`, `internal`), `err.status` and `err.message`.
On `401` the SDK sends the page to the login screen by itself.

{{.CheatSheet}}

Document ids allow only `A-Z a-z 0-9 _ - : .`; derive per-person ids with
`me.id.replace(/[^A-Za-z0-9_\-:.]/g, "_")`. Query values must have the same JSON type as the
field (`"3"` never equals `3`) and missing fields never match. There is no atomic increment:
store one document per vote/like and count them.

